tool_call: {"command": "kubectl get deployment frontend -n hotel-reservation -o yaml"} - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c692a8ad-3c45-4872-ba1d-4d9810cdf5c6'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nHotel Reservation\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nhotel-reservation\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f828da18-fe17-4af1-85b1-546084c76a9d'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='90e1b921-4d2b-4546-82e8-8f23799cb67c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s start by understanding the problem. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. The goal is to monitor and diagnose any faults in the system. The first step is to figure out what services are running in the cluster. Since the application is in the namespace \'hotel-reservation\', I need to get a list of all the services within that namespace.\n\nLooking at the available tools, the \'get_services\' tool from Grafana can retrieve the list of service names. However, the user mentioned that the application uses Kubernetes, so maybe checking the Kubernetes resources would be more direct. The \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' tool allows running kubectl commands. Using \'kubectl get services\' in the \'hotel-reservation\' namespace would list all the services. But the user hasn\'t specified if they have access to kubectl or if they should use Grafana. Since the tools provided include \'get_services\' for Grafana, perhaps that\'s the intended approach. However, Grafana might not list all services, especially if they\'re not exposed as metrics. Alternatively, using kubectl would give a more accurate list of services. But the user might not have access to the cluster directly. Hmm.\n\nWait, the \'get_services\' tool\'s description says it retrieves service names from Grafana. If Grafana is monitoring the cluster, this could include services that are being tracked. But maybe the user needs to first get the list of services in the namespace. Let me check the tools again. The \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' can run \'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation\' to list all services in that namespace. That would be more reliable. So, using the kubectl tool to get the services would be the first step. Then, once the services are known, further steps can be taken, like checking their metrics or traces. So the first tool to use is \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' with the command \'get services -n hotel-reservation\'.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: To identify all microservices running in the cluster, I need to first list all services in the hotel-reservation namespace using kubectl. This will provide the complete inventory of services, deployments, and pods, which is essential for diagnosing faults across the application. The command "kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation" will retrieve the list of services, while "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation" and "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" will provide additional context on their deployment status and running instances. \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 547, 'prompt_tokens': 1500, 'total_tokens': 2047, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfab4-66ae-7a72-affe-19ccd9f4ab35-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1500, 'output_tokens': 547, 'total_tokens': 2047}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='92f7552c-97ff-483e-8325-4828a9957e73'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s start by figuring out what the user needs. They want to monitor and diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first step is to identify all the services running in the cluster. The user mentioned using the hotel-reservation namespace.\n\nIn the previous step, the assistant chose the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with the command "kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation". Now, the user is asking for the tool call based on that choice. \n\nWait, the user\'s last message says to generate a tool call according to the last chosen tool. The last chosen tool was exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, and the command was "kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation". So the tool call should be formatted correctly as JSON within the tool_call tags.\n\nI need to make sure the JSON is properly structured with the command parameter. Also, check if there are any other commands needed, like getting deployments or pods, but the initial step was just services. The user might need more info later, but the first call is for services. So the correct tool call is the one with the services command.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9384130a78e1f9e0', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 263, 'prompt_tokens': 2540, 'total_tokens': 2803, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfab4-8110-74c3-8587-905497998969-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9384130a78e1f9e0', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2540, 'output_tokens': 263, 'total_tokens': 2803}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     TYPE           CLUSTER-IP       EXTERNAL-IP                                PORT(S)                               AGE\nconsul                   ClusterIP      10.109.74.163    <none>                                     8300/TCP,8400/TCP,8500/TCP,8600/UDP   76m\nfrontend                 ClusterIP      10.103.40.233    <none>                                     5000/TCP                              76m\ngeo                      ClusterIP      10.107.249.113   <none>                                     8083/TCP     