rror. So the root cause is likely that the container image is missing the required executable. \n\nBut how to confirm this? The user might need to check the image\'s contents. Since they can\'t do that directly, maybe they can check the deployment\'s YAML to see the command and image details. Using kubectl to get the deployment\'s YAML would help. So the next step is to retrieve the deployment configuration for the frontend service to check the image and command specified. That way, they can verify if the image is correct and if the command is properly set to run the frontend executable.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The frontend pod\'s container failed because the executable "frontend" was not found in the $PATH. This indicates the container image might be missing the required binary or the entry point/command in the deployment is misconfigured. To confirm, I need to inspect the deployment YAML for the **frontend** service to verify the image and command specified. This will clarify if the image is incorrect, the command is malformed, or the binary is missing from the image.  \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get deployment frontend -n hotel-reservation -o yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 638, 'prompt_tokens': 4886, 'total_tokens': 5524, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfab5-0623-70d3-ae2c-052a35e2b1f8-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4886, 'output_tokens': 638, 'total_tokens': 5524}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7fe4a025-4b7b-425a-b69a-fc984c59fee8'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user i