the commands, maybe the system has some history. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the get_previous_rollbackable_cmd is necessary to list the commands, then use rollback_command on each. But the user's task says to run the rollback tool (which is rollback_command) as many times as possible. So perhaps the correct first step is to call get_previous_rollbackable_cmd to find out which commands are available, then proceed to rollback each. \n\nBut the user's initial instruction says to run the rollback tool (rollback_command) until no more operations. So maybe the first step is to call rollback_command, but if there are no commands, it would just exit. However, without knowing the commands, maybe the system has some default. \n\nWait, the user's task says that the system is in a faulty state, and the previous mitigation steps may have caused issues. So the correct approach is to rollback all changes made in the previous steps. Therefore, the first step is to get the list of commands that can be rolled back. \n\nSo, the first tool to use is get_previous_rollbackable_cmd. That way, I can see which commands were executed and can then undo them in the correct order. \n\nTherefore, the correct tool choice is get_previous_rollbackable_cmd. Once I have that list, I can proceed to call rollback_command for each command in reverse order. But the user's instruction says to run the rollback tool as many times as possible. So maybe the initial step is to call get_previous_rollbackable_cmd to get the list, then use rollback_command multiple times. \n\nBut the user is in the thinking stage, so the first step is to choose the tool. The answer should be to call get_previous_rollbackable_cmd to retrieve the list of commands that can be rolled back. That way, I can determine which commands to undo. \n\nSo the tool choice is get_previous_rollbackable_cmd. The reasoning is that I need to know which commands were executed to roll them back. Without that information, I can't proceed effectively. \n\n